<%= render "shared/page_header",
      title: "What Elephants Eat",
      intro: "Elephants are herbivores. That means they only eat plants. Plants don't give them much energy, " \
             "so elephants spend most of the day eating!" %>

<% external_link = ->(label, url) { link_to label, url, target: "_blank", rel: "noopener", class: "text-link" } %>

<div class="section">
  <h2>How much do elephants eat?</h2>
  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat-card" data-mascot-fact="That&#x27;s like eating around 1,000 apples every day!">
      <div class="stat-label">Food per day</div>
      <div class="stat-value">100–150 kg</div>
      <p>That's about as heavy as two grown-up people!</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card" data-mascot-fact="Elephants chew with four huge molars, each about the size of a brick.">
      <div class="stat-label">Time spent eating</div>
      <div class="stat-value">12–18 hours</div>
      <p>Elephants snack all day and all night.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card" data-mascot-fact="Elephants don&#x27;t drink through their trunk like a straw. They suck water up, then squirt it into their mouth.">
      <div class="stat-label">Water per day</div>
      <div class="stat-value">Up to 200 L</div>
      <p>About a full bathtub! One trunkful can hold about 10 litres.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p>
      <strong>Why so much?</strong> Elephants only use less than half of the food they eat. The rest comes out as dung (poo).
      So they have to eat a lot to get enough energy.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>What's on the menu?</h2>
  <p>Click a food to learn more about it. These links go to another website, so ask a grown-up before you click.</p>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Food</th>
        <th>About it</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Grass", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poaceae") %></td>
        <td>The main food in the rainy season, when fresh grass grows everywhere.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Leaves and twigs", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acacia") %></td>
        <td>Pulled off trees and bushes, like acacia trees. Elephants eat more of these in the dry season.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Bark", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bark_(botany)") %></td>
        <td>Peeled off trees with their tusks. Bark is full of fibre and calcium, which keeps bones strong.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Roots", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root") %></td>
        <td>Dug up with feet and tusks when there isn't much food above the ground.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Fruit", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marula") %></td>
        <td>A favourite treat! Forest elephants eat lots of fruit, and African elephants love marula fruit.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Bamboo", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bamboo") %></td>
        <td>A big part of the menu for Asian elephants that live in forests.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><%= external_link.("Salt", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mineral_lick") %></td>
        <td>Licked off rocks or eaten in salty soil. Some elephants even walk into caves to find it!</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Diet through the seasons</h2>
  <div class="info-block" data-mascot-fact="Elephants love fermented marula fruit, but scientists say they&#x27;d have to eat an enormous amount to actually get drunk.">
    <p>
      In the rainy season, elephants mostly eat fresh grass. When it gets dry, the grass turns hard and brown.
      Then elephants eat more leaves, branches, bark and roots. Changing their food helps them get through long dry times.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Gardeners of the forest</h2>
  <div class="info-block" data-mascot-fact="Some seeds actually sprout better after a trip through an elephant&#x27;s gut.">
    <p>
      Lots of seeds go right through an elephant and come out whole in its dung (poo), sometimes far away from where they
      were eaten. The seeds then grow into new plants. Some trees need elephants to spread their seeds.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
